<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Badaruddin I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1757</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Badaruddin I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> from 1724 to 1757. His reign was the high point of the sultanate, enriched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangka">Bangka</a> sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. He built the Great Mosque of Palembang, which now bears his name.</p></div>
